  - q: "What should I wear for quad biking in Dubai?"
    a: "Closed shoes, trousers or shorts you do not mind getting sandy, a t-shirt, and sunglasses. We provide the helmet. Avoid sandals, flip-flops, loose scarves and anything that hangs. In the cooler months, or for any session finishing after sunset, add a light jacket or hoodie."
  - q: "Can I wear shorts on a quad bike or dune buggy?"
    a: "Yes, and plenty of people do. The trade-off is that sand sprays up and bare legs collect it, and a hot foot peg or a warm seat is less pleasant on skin. Long trousers are the more comfortable choice on a quad in particular. On the KTM 450 long trousers and ankle-covering shoes are strongly recommended rather than optional."
  - q: "Do I need special shoes?"
    a: "No, but they have to be closed. Trainers are ideal. Sandals and flip-flops are the single most common thing that causes a problem at the base, because you cannot work a foot pedal or brace on a peg in them, and sand at temperature is not something you want against bare feet."
  - q: "What should women wear for a desert ride in Dubai?"
    a: "The same practical list: closed shoes, comfortable trousers or leggings, a top with sleeves if you burn easily, sunglasses and a hair tie. Long loose hair and an open cockpit do not mix well, and a scarf is the one item we would ask you to leave behind because of the risk of it catching."
  - q: "Should I bring a jacket to the desert?"
    a: "Yes, for any session that ends after sunset, in any month. The desert loses heat quickly once the sun drops and the temperature difference between the drive out and the drive back surprises people every winter. It is the single most common thing guests wish they had brought."
  - q: "What about sunscreen and sunglasses?"
    a: "Sunscreen before you leave the hotel, because there is no shade in a dune field and the reflected light off pale sand does as much as the direct sun. Sunglasses are close to essential on a quad, which has no screen between you and the sand spray. In a buggy the screen takes most of it."
  - q: "Can I wear contact lenses?"
    a: "You can, and most people are fine, but fine airborne sand is harder on lenses than on glasses. If you have the option, glasses under the helmet visor are the easier choice for an open-desert session."
  - q: "What should I not bring?"
    a: "Loose scarves, dangling jewellery, a hat you intend to wear under the helmet, and anything in an open pocket. Phones come out at the photo stop rather than during the ride. Leave anything you would be upset to see covered in fine sand in the car."
