## The two items that actually matter

**Closed shoes.** Trainers are perfect. This is the one thing we will turn people away over, because a quad has foot pegs and a buggy has pedals, and neither works in a sandal. Hot sand against bare feet at a photo stop is the other half of the reason.

**Sunglasses.** More important on a quad than in a buggy, and the difference is the screen. A [dune buggy](/dune-buggy-dubai/) has one and it takes most of the sand spray. A [quad](/quad-bike-dubai/) does not, so you get the full amount of it at eye level.

Everything after this is comfort rather than a requirement.

## What the helmet changes

Helmets are ours, they are included in every price, and they are mandatory. Two consequences people do not think about until they are at the base.

Hats are pointless. Leave them at the hotel, because nothing goes on your head under a helmet.

Long hair needs tying back before the helmet goes on, not after. A hair tie takes no space and saves a genuinely uncomfortable hour.

## Clothing, by season

| Month | Daytime slot | After sunset |
|---|---|---|
| October to April | T-shirt and trousers | Add a jacket or hoodie, every time |
| May to September | Lightest thing you own | Still cooler than you expect |

The winter evening layer is the thing guests mention most often afterwards. The desert holds no heat once the sun drops, and the difference between a 4pm pickup and an 8pm return in January is large enough to change the mood of the whole trip.

In summer the sessions move to shortly after sunrise or late afternoon anyway, so the coldest you will be is still comfortable. What you are dressing for then is sun rather than temperature.

## Long trousers or shorts

Honest answer: shorts are fine and plenty of guests wear them.

Long trousers are the better choice on a quad, because sand sprays up from the front wheels and bare legs collect it for an hour. They are also better on any session on the [KTM 450](/ktm-dirt-bike-dubai/), where we recommend long trousers and ankle-covering shoes rather than leaving it to preference.

In a buggy it matters least, because you are seated behind a screen with a harness on.

## Children, and what changes for them

Nothing on the list changes except that it gets enforced harder. Closed shoes are the same rule at six as at forty, and a child in sandals will not ride.

The two additions worth packing are a spare top and a hair tie. Children end a session considerably sandier than adults do, because they are closer to the ground on the small quads and they enjoy the part where the sand comes up.

Sunglasses are harder to keep on a small face under a helmet. If they will not stay put, leave them, and book the earlier or later slot so the sun is lower.

## What to leave in the car

Loose scarves, above everything. A scarf near a moving machine with a spinning wheel is the one clothing item we would rather nobody brought at all.

Dangling jewellery, for the same reason. Anything in an open pocket, because it will be in the sand within ten minutes. And the phone, until the photo stop, which is what the photo stop is for.

## Sun, sand and the things nobody mentions

Put sunscreen on at the hotel rather than at the base. There is no shade in a dune field, and the light bouncing off pale sand does as much work as the sun directly overhead.

Fine sand gets into camera lenses, watch faces and zips. It is not damaging in an hour, but if you own something you would be upset to see dusted, it belongs in the car.

Take a change of shoes if you are going anywhere straight afterwards. Nothing gets destroyed, but you will be carrying sand for the rest of the evening.

## Getting there

Most guests drive themselves to our base at Al Awir on the Dubai-Hatta road, about 45 minutes from Downtown Dubai. There is parking on site and we send directions once the booking is confirmed, so that option costs nothing. Pickup and drop-off is available as an option on buggy, quad and dirt bike bookings at AED 300.

We have run this base since 2020 and taken more than 17,000 guests out. The ones who come back in the right shoes are the ones who enjoyed the first hour.
